meta {
  name: Upload Avatar
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

put {
  url: {{avatarUploadUrl}}
  body: text
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: image/png
}

body:text {
  not-a-real-png-but-storage-does-not-care
}

tests {
  test("storage accepts the upload", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
}

docs {
  # PUT <uploadUrl> — the raw upload (step 2 of 3)

  Not an API endpoint: this goes straight to object storage using the URL from
  `Presign Avatar Upload`. `auth: none` is load-bearing — a bearer header
  would break the S3 signature — and the `Content-Type` must match the signed
  one. See `Upload Quote Document` for the full story.

  The bytes are irrelevant to the API: the commit step reads back the object's
  **stored type and size** via `HeadObject`, which is why this stub passes as
  a "PNG" — the stored type is the signed header, not the content.
}
